"""
Long-form weapon lore for Edgerunner flavor models (inv/info).

Keyed by the exact weapon display string in WEAPON_FLAVOR_BY_QUALITY["name"].
Lookup uses normalize_weapon_label() so spacing/quotes variants still match.

Edit this file to change weapon prose; keys must match WEAPON_FLAVOR_BY_QUALITY names exactly.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

FLAVOR_LONG_LORE_BY_DISPLAY_NAME: dict[str, str] = {
    # --- Medium Pistol ---
    "Budget Arms \"Peashooter\"": "The Peashooter arrived in vending machines across Night City in a colorway Budget Arms describes as \"pacific teal,\" which is to say a green that inspires no confidence whatsoever. The barrel is marginally too short for the caliber, producing a muzzle flash disproportionate to the damage, and the grip panels have a tendency to loosen after extended use. Nobody who carries one is proud of it, but plenty of people are alive because they had it.",
    "Dai Lung Cybermag 8 \"Knockoff\"": "The Cybermag 8 is what happens when Dai Lung's quality control goes on a long lunch and doesn't come back. The slide finish is uneven, the reset on the trigger is imprecise, and the feed ramp has a relationship with hollow-points that can only be described as adversarial. It is, however, priced low enough that most buyers consider it disposable, which is the only honest framing for what it actually is.",
    "CCMMC Type-2 \"Gutter\"": "The Type-2 is Chinese military surplus stock that failed inspection and found its way into the civilian gray market, which is the kindest possible origin story for it. The finish is a flat olive drab that chips at the edges, the sights are drifted slightly right from the factory and have never been corrected, and the magazine release is stiff enough to require two hands. It technically fires in one direction, which is the floor for what constitutes a functional firearm.",
    "Street Iron \"Patchwork\"": "The Patchwork is exactly what the name suggests -- a pistol assembled from components sourced from at least three different donor weapons, held together by welds that are visible on the frame and a confidence in the builder that is either inspirational or delusional. The slide and frame are different shades of black, the grip wrap is electrical tape, and the barrel is mated to the slide with a friction fit that becomes exciting when the weapon heats up. It functions until it doesn't, which is true of everything, just more immediately so here.",
    "No-Name \"Throwaway\"": "The Throwaway has no manufacturer's mark, no serial number, and no history worth recording. It is a stamped-steel semi-automatic in an indeterminate caliber assembled somewhere in Night City's shadow economy, parkerized to prevent rust and wrapped in a grip cut from a rubber floor mat. The trigger pull is long and heavy in a way that suggests the designer was thinking about liability even while building an unregistered weapon. You use it once, maybe twice, and then you put it somewhere it will never be found.",
    "Federated Arms X-6 \"Sparrow\"": "The X-6 is a lightweight polymer-framed semi-automatic in a shade of grey that manages to look both mass-produced and vaguely apologetic. Federated Arms ships it in a blister pack with a single spare magazine and a warranty card that nobody fills out. It functions reliably enough that Night City's resale market stays permanently flooded with them, which tells you something about the average lifespan of their owners.",
    "Dai Lung Cybermag 15": "The Cybermag 15 is a compact Chinese-market semi-auto with a matte black finish that starts peeling around the grip after a few months of regular carry. Dai Lung cut costs on the tolerances during manufacture, and the result is a pistol that will feed most commercial ammunition most of the time, which is the most honest thing that can be said about it. It is, however, extremely cheap, and in Night City that counts for a great deal.",
    "Militech M-9 \"Garter\"": "The Garter is Militech's compact concealment pistol, designed for the sort of personnel who attend meetings where visible weapons are considered impolite. It has a slim profile, a flush-fit magazine, and a de-cocking lever positioned to be operated one-handed under a table. Militech does not advertise it widely, which is perhaps the most honest piece of marketing they've ever done.",
    "Sternmeyer P-14": "The P-14 is a no-frills German-engineered semi-automatic that has been in continuous production for decades because nobody has found a compelling reason to stop making it. It is heavier than most competitors, blued steel over a polymer frame, with fixed sights that are set at the factory and left alone. Sternmeyer's position is that a weapon requiring constant adjustment is a weapon that was never built correctly in the first place.",
    "Budget Arms \"Quickdraw\"": "The Quickdraw is sold through Budget Arms vending machines in a sealed polymer clamshell alongside two magazines and a printed card that says \"aim for center mass.\" The frame is a pale beige that Budget Arms calls \"urban sand\" in their catalog, and the action has a slightly springy quality that experienced shooters find unsettling. It works, mostly, which is exactly as much as Budget Arms promises.",
    "Malorian Arms 1050 \"Whisper\"": "The Whisper is Malorian's entry-level carry piece, which means it is still hand-fitted, match-grade, and more expensive than most people's monthly rent. The frame is a titanium alloy in a deep charcoal finish with a texture that manages to be grippy without being aggressive, and the action has been tuned to a trigger pull that breaks like a dry twig -- clean, consistent, and impossible to mistake for anything else. The name is accurate: the integrated compensator and tight barrel tolerances keep the report lower than the caliber has any right to produce.",
    "Arasaka \"Tsubame\"": "The Tsubame -- swallow, in Japanese -- is Arasaka's premium compact carry pistol, designed for personnel who need something that will pass for an accessory at a corporate function and perform like a weapon when required. The frame is a brushed ceramic-alloy composite in a pale grey that photographs as fashionable, and the slide is engraved with a subtle wave pattern that becomes visible only in direct light. What is not subtle is the precision of the action, which has been tuned to a standard that Arasaka's armorers won't put a number to but will defend in writing.",
    "Sternmeyer P-14 \"Praezision\"": "The Praezision is the P-14 rebuilt from specification as a precision instrument rather than a production firearm. Sternmeyer pulls the best frames from their line, fits a match-grade barrel by hand, and performs a trigger job that reduces the pull weight to a figure they only quote to clients who understand what it means. The external finish is identical to the standard P-14, which Sternmeyer considers a point of pride: the quality is in the function, not the advertising.",
    "Custom Works \"Surgeon\"": "Every Surgeon is different, built to the specification of a single client by a Night City custom shop that does not advertise and does not have a waiting list because it does not take new clients without a referral. The example most often seen in the field has a carbon-fiber frame, a bull barrel with an integrated compensator, and grips made from a material the builder describes only as \"something recovered from a corpo disposal site.\" The trigger breaks at a weight the owner refuses to disclose, and the accuracy is the kind that makes other shooters stop and ask questions.",
    "Tsunami Arms \"Koi\"": "The Koi is Tsunami's dress carry piece, designed for executives who attend the kind of events where weapons are not invited but threats arrive anyway. The frame is a slim aluminum alloy with a matte champagne finish, the slide is hand-engraved with a koi motif in a style understated enough to pass as decorative, and the internal components are fitted to tolerances that Tsunami publishes in their premium catalog with a confidence bordering on arrogance. It is a beautiful object that is also, without reservation, a serious weapon.",
    # --- Heavy Pistol ---
    "Budget Arms \"Stomper\"": "The Stomper is Budget Arms' attempt at the heavy pistol market, which involved taking their existing medium pistol platform, widening the frame, and hoping nobody looked closely at the engineering. The slide has a slight wobble in the frame that becomes more pronounced as the weapon wears, and the grip angle is wrong in a way that is hard to describe and impossible to ignore. It still fires, and it still hurts whatever it hits, which is the floor Budget Arms aims for and reliably clears.",
    "Dai Lung TP-4": "The TP-4 is Dai Lung's heavy pistol, a large-frame semi-automatic built to a price point that leaves very little budget for finishing work. The machining on the slide is visible under direct light, the trigger has a creep in it that never goes away regardless of how much the action is worked, and the finish is a flat black that manages to look both matte and greasy simultaneously. It is chambered in a caliber that does real damage, which is the only metric Dai Lung met without qualification.",
    "CCMMC \"Brickyard\"": "The Brickyard is a Chinese military surplus heavy pistol that arrived in the civilian market after failing acceptance testing for reasons the import documentation does not specify. It is heavy, blocky, and accurate in roughly the same way a thrown brick is accurate -- directionally correct, imprecise in the details. The grip is a textured black rubber that was glued on at some point after original manufacture, suggesting the original grips were lost or unusable, and the question of which story is less concerning is left as an exercise for the buyer.",
    "Street Forge \"Slag\"": "The Slag is a heavy pistol assembled in Night City's shadow manufacturing economy from reclaimed materials that were not originally intended to become a firearm. The frame appears to be cast rather than machined, with surface imperfections that the builder has filled with a grey compound of uncertain composition, and the barrel is a replacement unit from a different caliber that has been bored out to approximate compatibility. It shoots, and the rounds go forward, which is honestly more than the build quality suggests it should be able to promise.",
    "Rostovic TP-1 \"Jeftino\"": "Jeftino means cheap, and Rostovic named this weapon that in their internal catalog before they named it anything else, which is the most honest thing a firearms manufacturer has ever done. The TP-1 is a budget heavy pistol built on a deliberately simplified design -- fewer parts, looser tolerances, faster assembly -- sold to the lower end of the market without pretense. The finish is a thin parkerizing over mild steel, the sights are fixed and slightly optimistic, and the trigger feels like it was installed by someone working against a deadline. It works well enough to justify its price.",
    "Militech M-22 \"Warden\"": "The Warden is Militech's standard-issue sidearm for corporate security at the contractor tier -- the pistol handed to someone the day they sign a contract and taken back if they're ever fired. It's a full-size polymer-framed semi-automatic in a matte black finish with a rail under the barrel and three-dot sights that are adequate without being impressive. Hundreds of thousands of them exist, which means spare parts are easy to find, and Night City's gunsmiths can field-strip one in their sleep.",
    "Sternmeyer P-50": "The P-50 is the heavier end of Sternmeyer's pistol line, a full-steel-frame semi-automatic in a deep blue-black that carries weight in both senses of the word. It has a manual safety, a double-action first shot, and the kind of substantial heft that makes shooters either confident or cautious depending on their experience. Sternmeyer has been making variants of the P-50 platform for so long that the design has become less a product and more an institution.",
    "Constitutional Arms \"Bulldog\"": "The Bulldog is an American heavy pistol in the old tradition -- large, loud, and built on the assumption that the person holding it is probably right. It has a stainless steel slide over a polymer frame in a color Constitutional Arms calls \"liberty grey,\" a large external hammer, and a muzzle that makes its intentions clear without requiring documentation. It is not the most sophisticated weapon available, but sophistication was not the design brief.",
    "Arasaka \"Kensei\"": "The Kensei is named for a master swordsman, which is the kind of naming decision that either lands or doesn't depending on whether the weapon justifies the comparison. In this case it does: the Kensei is a slim, elegant semi-automatic with a ceramic-alloy frame in a brushed anthracite, fitted sights, and an action that breaks cleanly at a weight that suggests someone at Arasaka spent real time on it. It is priced for officers, not grunts, and it performs accordingly.",
    "Rostovic TP-9 \"Grom\"": "The Grom is a Serbian-manufactured heavy pistol with an aesthetic that can generously be described as industrial and more accurately as brutal. The frame is a reinforced polymer in a dark grey, the slide is machined steel with deep serrations, and the muzzle brake is added not for precision but because the caliber demands it. Rostovic named it after thunder, which is appropriate: it is loud, blunt, and arrives without much warning.",
    "Malorian Arms 2020": "The 2020 is the pistol that established Malorian Arms' reputation and has spent decades defending it. It is a full-steel semi-automatic with a hand-fitted barrel, a trigger job performed by Malorian's senior armorers, and a finish so deep and even that it functions as a mirror in the right light. The grips are a dark stabilized wood over rubber, the sights are adjustable night-sights, and the whole assembly communicates a single message: this weapon was built by people who care about the work.",
    "Arasaka \"Kensei-X\"": "The Kensei-X is the extended precision variant of the standard Kensei line, built for Arasaka field officers who need a sidearm that performs at ranges where heavy pistols are not supposed to be competitive. The barrel is extended and threaded, the frame has been lightened at the dust cover, and the sights are tall suppressor-height adjustables in a tritium-illuminated configuration. It is issued with a proof target from the test session at Arasaka's internal range, signed by the armorer who fitted it.",
    "Sternmeyer P-50 \"Adler\"": "The Adler -- eagle -- is the P-50 rebuilt as a match pistol that happens to be carried into combat. Sternmeyer fits a match-grade barrel by hand, performs a complete trigger job to a published specification, and installs adjustable target sights in a configuration borrowed from their competition pistol line. The external finish is a deep phosphate black over steel, and the grip panels are a stippled G-10 in black, and the result is a weapon that looks like a serious tool and shoots like one too.",
    "Constitutional Arms \"Arbiter Elite\"": "The Arbiter Elite is what Constitutional Arms produces when they stop thinking about price points and start thinking about reputation. The frame is hand-fitted to the slide, the barrel is match-grade with a target crown, and the trigger breaks at a pull weight that Constitutional Arms' custom shop specifies to the tenth of a pound. The grips are optional walnut or G-10, the finish is a hand-applied blue so deep it looks black until the light catches it, and the whole package arrives in a fitted Pelican case with two magazines and a test target.",
    "Custom Works \"Iron Covenant\"": "The Iron Covenant is a Night City bespoke commission piece built by a fabricator who works entirely by referral and whose backlog runs to fourteen months. Each one is unique in the details -- different frame treatments, different sight configurations, different trigger weights -- but all share the builder's philosophy, which is that no specification is final until the client is satisfied. The example most frequently seen is a hard-chromed full-steel pistol with a flared magwell and a trigger pull that the owner describes, with visible pleasure, as \"the best I've ever touched.\"",
    # --- Very Heavy Pistol ---
    "Budget Arms \"Wallbreaker\"": "The Wallbreaker is Budget Arms' very heavy pistol, which they appear to have designed by asking what the largest gun was that could still technically be called a pistol and then building something slightly worse than that. The frame is an oversized version of their standard polymer construction, visibly flexing under recoil, and the muzzle brake is a cast unit that is attached by two screws and should be checked before every firing session. It does, however, hit very hard, which Budget Arms has correctly identified as the one non-negotiable requirement for this category.",
    "Dai Lung HVP-1": "The HVP-1 is Dai Lung's attempt at the very heavy pistol market, which required them to operate at a scale they were not fully prepared for. The frame is too large for their existing tooling, producing a grip that most human hands cannot reach the trigger on comfortably, and the slide has a disconcerting amount of play in the frame that increases with every hundred rounds fired. It is chambered correctly and achieves its published velocity, which Dai Lung considers a full success and their customers consider the bare minimum.",
    "Street Forge \"Anchor\"": "The Anchor is named for two things: what it feels like in your hand, and what it does to your draw speed. It is a Night City shadow-economy very heavy pistol built on a frame that appears to have been cast from a material with a higher specific gravity than is strictly necessary, fitted with a barrel of uncertain provenance, and finished with a coat of flat black spray that chips at the edges. It shoots large bullets in a roughly forward direction, and at close range -- the only range that matters for something this unwieldy -- it does exactly what a very heavy pistol is supposed to do.",
    "CCMMC \"Sledge\"": "The Sledge is a Chinese military reject very heavy pistol that the original procurement board declined to accept, a decision that becomes self-explanatory upon handling. It is enormous, imbalanced toward the muzzle in a way that makes one-handed shooting an act of commitment, and the double-action trigger pull is heavy enough to provide resistance training as a secondary benefit. The caliber is correct and the rounds exit at appropriate velocities, but everything about the experience of firing it suggests the design process was conducted under time pressure.",
    "Gutter Works \"Last Resort\"": "The Last Resort is named with precision rather than irony: it is what you pick up when everything else is gone or broken, and the name exists to remind you of that context so you do not form inappropriate attachments. It is a crude large-frame semi-automatic built from Night City scrap, with a grip wrapped in foam rubber secured with hose clamps, a barrel that has been bored from steel pipe, and a firing mechanism that functions on the principle that enough spring tension will resolve most design problems. It will fire. It will probably not fail immediately. That is the complete warranty.",
    "Militech M-38 \"Brute\"": "The Brute is Militech's answer to the question of how much gun a single hand can reasonably manage, and their answer is: more than you'd expect. It is a large-frame semi-automatic with a ported barrel, an integrated recoil buffer in the grip, and a muzzle that is difficult to look at from the wrong direction without reconsidering your life choices. Militech ships it with a single magazine, a wrist strap, and a warning label that is not ironic.",
    "Sternmeyer T-80": "The T-80 is Sternmeyer's heavy-frame pistol, a large semi-automatic built on a platform that their engineers describe as \"appropriate for the caliber\" and their marketing team has diplomatically chosen not to put a picture of in most print advertisements. The frame is reinforced polymer over a steel chassis, the slide is milled from a single block of tool steel, and the recoil spring is a dual-unit configuration that is the only reason the shooter's wrist survives sustained use. It is heavy, serious, and extremely accurate for something this size.",
    "Arasaka \"Raijin\"": "Raijin is the Japanese god of thunder, and the naming team at Arasaka got this one right. The Raijin is a large-frame semi-automatic with a ventilated barrel shroud, an angular slide with deep lightning-bolt serrations, and a grip texture that is almost aggressive in its thoroughness. The muzzle flash is significant enough that Arasaka includes a note in the documentation about eye protection, which most people ignore until the first time they fire it indoors.",
    "Malorian Arms 2040": "The 2040 is Malorian's standard very heavy pistol offering, a full-steel large-frame semi-automatic built to the same obsessive standard as everything else in their catalog. The action is hand-fitted, the barrel is match-grade, and the finish is a deep charcoal Cerakote applied over a polished steel surface that is smooth enough to reflect the shooter's face. It is priced at a level that makes most buyers pause, and most of them buy it anyway.",
    "Constitutional Arms \"Vindicator\"": "Constitutional Arms markets the Vindicator to the private citizen market with copy that suggests home defense applications, which technically includes every application anyone has ever used it for. It is a large-frame revolver chambered in a caliber that Constitutional Arms' brochure describes as \"decisive,\" with a four-inch ported barrel, rubber combat grips, and a cylinder that holds six rounds that are each, individually, more than enough. The Vindicator is sold legally in most jurisdictions, which says something about those jurisdictions.",
    "Malorian Arms 3500": "The 3500 occupies the position just below Malorian's legendary top-tier piece, which means it is still a hand-fitted, match-grade, full-steel semi-automatic that most manufacturers would be proud to put at the very top of their catalog. The barrel is a match unit hand-lapped to the frame, the action is tuned to a trigger pull that Malorian specifies in writing and backs with a guarantee, and the finish is a deep Cerakote over polished steel that is, objectively, beautiful. Malorian includes a test target and a certificate of armorer inspection with every 3500, and the test targets are worth framing.",
    "Arasaka \"Raijin-X\"": "The Raijin-X is the extended match variant of the standard Raijin, built for Arasaka's internal security personnel at the tier that handles problems too sensitive for conventional corp security. The barrel is extended four inches over standard, threaded and ported, and the frame has been lightened at the dust cover and trigger guard with material removed and weight redistributed to the grip for balance. The slide serrations are deeper on the Raijin-X, the sights are tall adjustable night-sights, and the whole assembly arrives in a fitted case with a test target, a suppressor adapter, and no documentation that would survive a legal inquiry.",
    "Sternmeyer T-80 \"Panzer\"": "Sternmeyer does not name most of their firearms, considering alphanumeric designations sufficient and evocative names slightly undignified. The Panzer is the exception, and the name was earned: it is the T-80 frame rebuilt entirely from premium components, with a match barrel hand-fitted to the slide, a trigger job performed by Sternmeyer's senior pistol armorer, and a finish so carefully applied that the frame and slide appear to have been machined from a single piece of steel. The grip panels are a G-10 composite over a precisely stippled steel backstrap, and the whole assembly weighs slightly more than a standard T-80 and shoots considerably better.",
    "Custom Works \"Deathwish\"": "The original Deathwish was built for a Solo who left very specific written requirements for the weapon and an equally specific instruction that if they were ever killed, the gun was to be destroyed rather than sold. The builder, who has a sense of drama to match their skill, chose instead to build a second one and call it the same name, which has become the shop's signature very heavy pistol commission. Each Deathwish is unique -- the first had a titanium frame with a deep anodized finish, the current iteration is a full-steel affair with a custom compensator and a grip texture the builder calls \"unforgiving\" -- but all share the trigger work, the barrel fitting, and the name.",
    "Tsunami Arms \"Orochi\"": "The Orochi is named for the eight-headed serpent of Japanese mythology, which Tsunami chose because the weapon has eight rounds in the magazine and because the name suggests both scale and menace. It is a large-frame semi-automatic with a ceramic-composite frame in Tsunami's signature dark grey, a match-grade ported barrel, and an action that the company's documentation describes only as \"within specification\" -- a phrase that, coming from Tsunami's precision division, means something considerably more impressive than it sounds. It ships with Tsunami's standard certificate of inspection and an ammunition recommendation that is, diplomatically, a warning.",
    # --- SMG ---
    "Budget Arms \"Slaught-O-Matic Junior\"": "The Junior is Budget Arms' single-use SMG, a smaller and cheaper variant of the already-minimal Slaught-O-Matic, sold in a sealed polymer sleeve from vending machines in the Combat Zones. It has a 20-round magazine capacity, a rate of fire that empties that magazine in approximately one second, and no provision for reloading because Budget Arms determined that the cost of a magazine release mechanism was not justified by the price point. When empty, the Junior is slightly more aerodynamic than it is functional, which is perhaps its most reliable property.",
    "Dai Lung Tempest 10": "The Tempest 10 is the bottom of the Tempest line, distinguished from the Tempest 30 primarily by a reduced magazine capacity and a further reduction in quality control that was not thought to be possible until it was achieved. The drum magazine included with the Tempest 10 holds 20 rounds and feeds them at an inconsistent rate that experienced shooters describe as \"negotiating with the weapon.\" The frame has visible casting seams and the barrel is a replacement unit from a different Dai Lung model that fits with enough friction to stay installed, usually.",
    "CCMMC \"Scattershot\"": "The Scattershot is a Chinese military reject SMG that appears to have failed inspection specifically on accuracy grounds, which is ironic given that accuracy in an SMG is less critical than in most weapon types and the CCMMC still couldn't meet the standard. The sights are drifted left from the factory, the barrel has been crowned in a way that suggests the tool was dull, and the fire selector has three positions of which the third is a mystery. It fires rapidly in a forward direction and at close range this is sufficient.",
    "Street Fab \"Chatter\"": "The Chatter is named for the sound it makes rather than what it does, which tells you something about the priorities of the person who named it. It is a Night City shadow-economy SMG built from stamped steel components, assembled without access to proper tooling, with a receiver that produces a disconcerting vibration during automatic fire. The magazine is a double-stack box type that fits loosely in the well and should be held in place by the support hand during firing. It is cheap, it is available, and in a very tight spot it is better than nothing.",
    "No-Name \"Garbage Fire\"": "The Garbage Fire did not receive this name from a manufacturer because it does not have a manufacturer, only an assembler who is no longer findable. It is a collection of SMG components that have been combined into something that functions as an SMG by any mechanical definition and by no other standard. The receiver is cracked at the ejection port and has been repaired with what appears to be automotive body filler. The magazine is secured with a strip of metal bent into a catch by someone who understood the principle but not the practice. It fires, which is the only thing anyone who touches it is hoping for.",
    "Militech M-14 \"Jackal\"": "The Jackal is the SMG that Militech distributes to second-tier contractors, private security details, and anyone else who needs automatic fire capability at a price that fits a line-item budget. It is a polymer-framed, blowback-operated compact SMG in a flat dark earth that has become so common in Night City that seeing one is unremarkable. The Jackal functions reliably on a wide range of ammunition, accepts standard suppressor threading, and is boring in every way that a working weapon should be boring.",
    "Arasaka WMA \"Kaze-5\"": "The Kaze-5 -- fifth-generation Wind -- is Arasaka's mid-tier SMG, a compact polymer-framed automatic with a folding forward grip and an ambidextrous fire selector that operates with one thumb. It is lighter than its size suggests, feeds reliably, and empties a magazine at a rate that makes controlled bursts a matter of discipline rather than mechanics. The finish is Arasaka's standard matte anthracite, the sights are a fixed post-and-notch, and the overall impression is of a weapon that has been designed to work rather than impress.",
    "Federated Arms X-12 \"Courier\"": "The Courier is Federated Arms' SMG offering, a compact blowback-operated automatic built light enough to run with and accurate enough to matter at the ranges where SMGs are relevant. The frame is a pale grey polymer with a rubber overmold on the grip, the charging handle is ambidextrous, and the included single-point sling is a thoughtful addition that most SMG manufacturers don't bother with. It is not exceptional, but it is reliable, and in Night City reliability is the more useful quality.",
    "Dai Lung Tempest 30": "The Tempest 30 is Dai Lung's high-capacity SMG, a blowback-operated spray weapon built around a 50-round drum magazine that is included in the box and is the weapon's primary selling point. The finish is a cheap phosphate black, the fire selector is stiff and requires positive pressure to engage, and the sights are a fixed ramp configuration calibrated optimistically. It cycles quickly and runs until something works loose, which takes longer than you'd expect given the construction quality.",
    "Budget Arms \"Slapdash\"": "The Slapdash is Budget Arms' SMG, sold through their vending machine network alongside a single magazine, a cleaning rod, and a card that reads \"aim for the middle.\" The receiver is a stamped steel construction in a grey primer finish that Budget Arms apparently decided was the final coat, the stock folds in a way that is supposed to improve concealment but mostly makes the weapon harder to shoot accurately, and the Slapdash has a rate of fire high enough that accuracy is somewhat optional. It works until it doesn't, and until then it works just fine.",
    "Militech M-14 \"Jackal-S\"": "The Jackal-S is the special operations variant of the standard Jackal, produced in limited numbers for Militech's Tier-1 contractor support program and not available through standard procurement channels. The barrel is match-grade and internally threaded for Militech's proprietary suppressor, the action has been tuned for a reduced fire rate that sacrifices some speed for significantly improved controllability, and the receiver has been lightened at the handguard and trigger guard. It comes with three magazines, the suppressor, and a test target, and it is extraordinarily good at the very specific job it was built for.",
    "Arasaka WMA \"Kaze-9 Custom\"": "The Kaze-9 Custom is what the Kaze-5 becomes when Arasaka's internal armory builds one to the specifications of a senior field operative rather than to a production standard. The action is tuned for single-shot semi-automatic capability in addition to the standard burst modes, the barrel is match-grade with a target crown, and the sights are replaced with a folding iron sight unit that cowitnesses with a standard micro optic. Arasaka does not sell these; they issue them to specific personnel, and they know where each one is at all times.",
    "Tsunami Arms \"Hayabusa\"": "The Hayabusa -- peregrine falcon -- is Tsunami's production premium SMG, their answer to the question of what a submachine gun looks like when you stop treating it as a budget category. The frame is a carbon-fiber reinforced polymer in Tsunami's dark grey, the barrel is a match unit with a proprietary muzzle brake, and the feed system has been redesigned from the standard Kaze architecture to accept Tsunami's high-capacity flush-fit magazines. It is fast, light, and accurate past the ranges where most SMGs stop pretending, and it costs enough to make that performance feel appropriate.",
    "Sternmeyer K-10 \"Praezision\"": "The Praezision is what happens when Sternmeyer's competition pistol division takes an interest in a submachine gun project. The trigger mechanism has been replaced entirely with a unit borrowed from their target pistol line, producing a reset and pull that SMG shooters describe as disorienting until they realize it's improving their splits between shots. The barrel is hand-fitted, the feed ramp is polished, and the whole assembly is tested at the factory with three different ammunition types before it is certified. It is, by a meaningful margin, the best-shooting SMG in its category.",
    "Custom Works \"Needle\"": "The Needle is made by a single Night City craftsperson who takes four commissions per year, has a waiting list, and charges more than the waiting list suggests is reasonable. The frame is a hand-machined aluminum alloy, the barrel is a custom-ordered match unit fitted by hand, and the trigger mechanism is a custom design with a pull the builder calls \"intentional\" -- meaning that every shot is deliberate, and that the weapon will not fire before the shooter means it to. The name comes from the precision it expects of the person holding it, and the precision it returns in kind.",
    # --- Heavy SMG ---
    "Budget Arms \"Bonebreaker\"": "The Bonebreaker is Budget Arms' heavy SMG, a product that should not exist at this price point but does because Budget Arms found a way to build it and someone found a way to buy it. The recoil is disproportionate to the caliber because the buffer system is undersized, the barrel heats to uncomfortable levels after a single magazine of sustained fire, and the rate of fire selector is technically three positions but functionally two because the middle position doesn't reliably engage. It is, however, chambered in a heavy caliber that does real damage, and at close range the accuracy requirements are forgiving.",
    "CCMMC TM-2 \"Rattle\"": "The Rattle earned its name from the sound the action makes during the first magazine of every firing session, which mechanics who have worked on it describe as \"the weapon warming up\" and everyone else describes as \"alarming.\" It is a Chinese military reject heavy SMG, a large-frame open-bolt automatic that passed out of service for reasons the import documentation specifies as \"internal process review,\" which is the kind of language used when nobody wants to write the real reason. It functions once it has cycled enough rounds to knock the manufacturing debris out of the action, which is a design quirk that requires a specific kind of patience.",
    "Dai Lung Stormfront 5": "The Stormfront 5 is the budget entry in Dai Lung's heavy SMG line, a large-frame automatic built to a price point that required eliminating several components that most heavy SMG manufacturers consider non-optional. The feed system is a single-stack design in a weapon that was clearly intended for a double-stack magazine, producing intermittent feeding issues with certain ammunition profiles, and the fire rate selector has been simplified to two positions -- safe and very fast -- on the assumption that the buyer is not interested in nuance. The finish is a thin phosphate black over what Dai Lung's factory floor calls \"grade-B steel,\" and everyone else calls \"the stuff left over.\"",
    "Street Forge \"Grinder\"": "The Grinder is a Night City shadow-economy heavy SMG built from parts that were not designed for each other, combined by an assembler who understood the principle of automatic weapons operation well enough to make it work without necessarily understanding why it works. The receiver is fabricated from two sections welded together, the barrel is a replacement unit from a light machine gun that has been trimmed to fit, and the fire rate is whatever the combination of spring weights and bolt mass produces, which is fast enough. The magazine fits with a satisfying click that is, given everything else, a surprising quality touch.",
    "Gutter Works \"Repurposed\"": "The Repurposed is named with the kind of honesty that is more common in Night City's black market than in its corporate boardrooms: this weapon was previously something else, and that something else is not documented. The frame has been modified at the receiver to accept a heavy SMG barrel, the trigger group is from a different weapon entirely, and the magazine well has been opened up to accept a magazine that is not originally compatible with any of the donor weapons. That it functions as a heavy SMG is either a testament to the builder's ingenuity or an argument against over-specifying what a weapon needs to be to meet the definition.",
    "Militech M-48 \"Hellhound\"": "The Hellhound is Militech's heavy SMG, a high-cyclic-rate automatic built for situations where the intent is suppression rather than precision. It runs hot -- Militech's documentation recommends a barrel change after every 300 rounds of sustained fire -- and it eats through ammunition at a rate that makes logistics personnel visibly uncomfortable. It is, within those constraints, an extremely effective weapon, and Militech sells it to anyone with a security contract and a materials budget that accounts for the ammunition consumption.",
    "Arasaka TKI-15 \"Arashi\"": "The Arashi is Arasaka's heavy SMG, a compact high-caliber automatic with a folding stock, a rail system, and a fire selector with three positions: safe, semi, and a burst mode that Arasaka's documentation describes as \"aggressive.\" The frame is Arasaka's standard anthracite polymer, the barrel is fluted for heat dissipation, and the whole weapon has a dense, purposeful quality that Arasaka's designers achieve by never including anything that isn't serving a function. It is more accurate than its fire rate suggests it has any right to be.",
    "Chadran Arms \"Block Warden\"": "The Block Warden is Chadran Arms' flagship heavy SMG, a large-frame automatic built with the kind of branding confidence that requires either excellent marketing or excellent engineering to justify. In this case it is the engineering: the Block Warden has a robust feeding system, a barrel heavy enough to resist deformation under sustained fire, and an ergonomics package that puts the controls exactly where experienced automatic weapons shooters expect them to be. The name is excessive, but the weapon is not.",
    "Sternmeyer K-30": "The K-30 is the heavy end of Sternmeyer's submachine gun line, a blowback-operated heavy caliber automatic that Sternmeyer builds with the same philosophy they apply to everything: correct tolerances, quality materials, and no ornamentation that doesn't serve a purpose. The receiver is a machined aluminum alloy, the barrel is a chrome-lined unit designed for extended use, and the controls are precise enough that manipulating them without looking is a natural skill development rather than a practiced one. It does not have a dramatic name because Sternmeyer does not believe dramatic names improve ballistic performance.",
    "Rostovic TM-7 \"Klanje\"": "Klanje means slaughter, which is a product name that Rostovic's marketing team apparently reviewed and approved without objection. The TM-7 is a heavy caliber open-bolt automatic built on Rostovic's standard tough-and-cheap philosophy, with a stamped steel receiver, a grip of dense black rubber, and a rate of fire high enough that the suppression effect begins the moment the trigger is depressed. It is crude in the details and effective in the outcome, which is the Rostovic design brief stated plainly.",
    "Militech M-48 \"Hellhound-S\"": "The Hellhound-S is the special operations variant of the standard Hellhound, which Militech produces in limited numbers for specific contract requirements and does not list in their standard catalog. The barrel is a heavy match unit with improved rifling that tightens the burst pattern at range, the buffer system has been redesigned to reduce the fire rate slightly in exchange for a dramatic improvement in controllability, and the heat signature has been reduced enough that thermal detection at distance becomes genuinely difficult. It ships with four magazines, a suppressor adapter, and a Militech-certified test target that makes the standard Hellhound look like a different weapon.",
    "Arasaka TKI-15 \"Arashi Custom\"": "The Arashi Custom is not available through any catalog. It is built by Arasaka's internal armory for specific operational personnel, hand-tuned to the individual who will carry it based on a specification process that involves a range session with an Arasaka armorer and a conversation that the armorer describes as a \"technical interview.\" The action is tuned to that shooter's preferred burst rate, the sights are zeroed to their dominant eye, and the grip panels are fitted to their hand dimensions. It is the best version of a very good weapon, and Arasaka does not loan them out.",
    "Chadran Arms \"Block Warden Executive\"": "The Executive is the version of the Block Warden that Chadran Arms builds when a client is willing to pay for the weapon the design brief described before the budget constraints arrived. The receiver is machined rather than cast, the barrel is a match-grade heavy unit with improved rifling, and the feed system has been redesigned with a stronger spring and a polished feed ramp that accepts every commercial ammunition profile without complaint. The name is ironic only if you're not the one holding it.",
    "Sternmeyer K-30 \"Sturmadler\"": "The Sturmadler -- storm eagle -- is the K-30 built to the specification that Sternmeyer's engineers wrote before the production budget was applied, and it represents what the K-30 would be if cost were not a consideration. The receiver is machined from a billet of aerospace aluminum, the barrel is a match unit with a chrome-lined bore and a competition crown, and the trigger mechanism has been replaced with a unit from Sternmeyer's precision rifle division. It is heavier than the standard K-30 and it shoots like the difference in weight was spent correctly.",
    "Tsunami Arms \"Ryujin\"": "The Ryujin -- dragon king -- is the flagship heavy SMG in Tsunami's premium lineup, a weapon they build in small batches and distribute through channels that require a security clearance or a very well-connected fixer to access. The frame is a carbon-fiber reinforced composite over an aluminum chassis, the barrel is a match unit with Tsunami's proprietary porting, and the fire selector has a fourth position that Tsunami's documentation describes as \"sustained operations mode\" and that everyone who has used it describes as \"exactly as much gun as you could want.\" It is beautiful, expensive, and unambiguously effective.",
    # --- Bow ---
    "Nomad Scrap Recurve": "The Nomad Scrap Recurve is the category name for bows assembled in Badlands camps from whatever materials were available, built by people who needed a bow and couldn't wait or travel for a better option. The riser is commonly bent rebar wrapped in tape for grip, the limbs are fiberglass strip salvaged from industrial shelving, and the string is cable, paracord, or climbing rope depending on what was on hand. They draw inconsistently, release honestly, and will kill game and people at distances where precision is not the limiting factor.",
    "Gutter Works \"Twig\"": "The Twig is a Night City-made bow assembled from construction materials by someone who understood the physics of archery in theory and applied them in practice with variable results. The limbs are PVC conduit stuffed with fiberglass batting for stiffness, the riser is a section of aluminum channel, and the arrow rest is a zip tie bent into position. It draws, it releases, and the arrow goes forward at a velocity that depends on which Twig you have, because no two are built the same way.",
    "Backyard Compound \"Cobble\"": "The Cobble is a homemade compound bow built by someone who understood that compound bows are more efficient than recurves and attempted to reproduce the mechanism from reference images. The cams are not machined -- they are routed from aluminum plate with a tool that was not originally intended for that purpose -- and the cable system is functional in good weather and creative in wet or cold conditions. The draw weight varies with temperature and string stretch, which experienced archers call a challenge and honest people call a liability.",
    "Street Fab \"Stick and String\"": "This is the designation written on a strip of electrical tape applied to the grip of a bow that has no other identifying information and requires none, because the name is a complete technical specification. The limbs are wood -- specifically, a length of wood that was identified as having appropriate flex characteristics through a process of empirical testing that involved breaking several lengths of wood -- and the string is a braided cord that is replaced whenever it shows wear. It shoots. It shoots adequately. This is not nothing.",
    "Discount Fletch \"Bargain Draw\"": "Discount Fletch is a budget archery supplier operating primarily in the outer districts, and the Bargain Draw is their lowest price point bow, sold complete with four arrows and a broadhead selection that has seen better days. The riser is a cast aluminum that flexes more than aluminum should flex, the limbs are a fiberglass composite of ambiguous origin, and the included arrow rest is a plastic shelf type that requires adjustment after approximately twenty shots. The warranty is printed on the receipt and is two sentences long, neither of which constitutes a meaningful guarantee.",
    "Compound Ghost": "The Ghost is a tactical compound bow with carbon-fiber limbs, an aluminum riser, and a draw weight adjustable between 40 and 70 pounds via a hex key that is stored in the grip. It is nearly silent at full draw, produces no electronic signature, and folds to a configuration that fits in a dedicated gear bag that is sold separately at a price that seems unreasonable until you need it. Preferred by runners who work in environments where electronic detection is a concern, the Ghost is the kind of weapon that announces itself only when it's too late for that to be useful information.",
    "Cascade Arms \"Heron\"": "The Heron is a folding takedown bow designed for couriers, travelers, and anyone who needs a ranged weapon option that does not attract attention in a bag scan. The riser breaks into two sections at a central joint, the limbs detach with a quarter-turn, and the whole assembly fits in a case the size of a laptop bag. Reassembly takes under a minute with practice, which Cascade Arms documents with a helpful instructional video that never actually shows anyone reassembling it under pressure.",
    "Ragged Whisper": "The Ragged Whisper is not a brand; it's a type -- the name that Night City's fixer community applies to any nomad-crafted recurve bow built from salvaged materials. The specific materials vary: riser sections of repurposed composite from vehicle components, limbs of layered fiberglass cut from industrial stock, serving wound from fishing line or paracord. No two are identical, each carries the marks of the hands that built it, and the ones that reach the city from the Badlands are usually the best ones, the ones that were used for years before they were sold.",
    "SilentField RX-3": "The RX-3 is a compound bow marketed to sport hunters and tactical enthusiasts, which in practice means it was designed by people who understand bows well and purchased by people across a very wide range of applications. It has a let-off of 80%, a draw cycle that is smooth enough for extended hold, and a sight mount that accepts any standard archery sight. The finish is a flat black that works adequately in tactical contexts and looks slightly out of place at the archery range, which perfectly describes its intended customer.",
    "Thornwood Composite": "The Thornwood Composite is sold through exactly three fixers in Night City, priced well above commercial compound bows, and built by a craftsperson in the Pacifica area whose name circulates in specific communities. The riser is a laminated wood-carbon hybrid, the limbs are a fiberglass-carbon composite, and every component is installed and tested by the same person who builds it. You wait several weeks, you pay in full, and what arrives is the kind of bow that people who care about bows look at slowly before asking where it came from.",
    "Cascade Arms \"Phantom\"": "The Phantom is Cascade Arms' premium tactical compound bow, the version they build when the production budget is not the primary concern. The riser is machined from a single billet of aerospace aluminum, the limbs are a carbon nanotube composite that is lighter and stiffer than any commercial fiberglass equivalent, and the cam system is designed for virtually zero vibration on release -- the arrow leaves the string before the frame has moved enough to affect the shot. It is quiet in a way that experienced shooters describe as unsettling until they understand what it means for their work.",
    "Thornwood \"Obsidian Series\"": "The Obsidian Series is the top of the Thornwood builder's output, a line of bows that are individually numbered, certified in writing by the builder, and accompanied by a draw cycle card showing the actual force-draw measurements of that specific bow. The riser is a carbon-fiber laminate over a titanium core, the limbs are a custom composite that the builder has not disclosed to anyone, and the string is a high-modulus polyethylene unit that is replaced at the builder's shop on a schedule specified in the purchase agreement. Three people in Night City own one. They know each other.",
    "SilentField RX-9 \"Ghost\"": "The RX-9 Ghost is SilentField's precision tactical variant, built for the segment of their market that moved from the RX-3 and wanted to know what a real budget looked like in bow form. The draw cycle has been redesigned for a longer valley and a cleaner back wall, the let-off is adjustable between 75% and 90%, and the integrated vibration dampening system reduces the limb noise on release to a level that SilentField publishes in their documentation as a decibel measurement, which no other bow manufacturer has ever done. The sight mount accepts a full-size optic without adapter.",
    "Custom Works \"Stillwater\"": "The Stillwater is a Night City bespoke bow commission built by a craftsperson who came to bow-building from precision engineering and has never entirely left. The riser is a hand-machined titanium alloy in a matte grey, the limbs are a carbon composite built to the shooter's draw length and preferred weight, and the cam geometry has been tuned to produce a specific draw cycle that the builder discusses with the client at length before beginning work. Three months from commission to delivery, payment in full at signing, and the result is a bow that the people who receive it treat as the most valuable object they own.",
    "Nomad Craft \"Elder Make\"": "An Elder Make is not bought; it is received, and only from the hands of the person who built it. These are bows made by Nomad craftspeople with decades of practice, built from the best materials available in their caravan's stores, and given -- not sold -- to someone the maker has decided deserves one. The construction is traditional recurve with modern materials where they're available and proven materials where they're not, and each one carries the subtle fingerprints of its maker's philosophy in the tiller, the string height, the grip shape. Night City fixers have offered significant sums for Elder Makes that passed through the market. The offers are usually declined.",
    # --- Crossbow ---
    "Gutter Works \"Bolt Chucker\"": "The Bolt Chucker acquired its name in the Night City resale market, and the original builder either does not know this or has accepted it with equanimity. It is a Night City-fabricated crossbow with a prod made from PVC and fiberglass strips, a stock that is a repurposed rifle buttstock with the wrong geometry for a crossbow application, and a bolt retention system that requires the shooter to hold the bolt in place until the trigger is pressed. That the bolt retention is a finger is not specified in any documentation, but becomes apparent on first use.",
    "CCMMC CB-1": "The CB-1 is a Chinese military surplus crossbow that was apparently rejected from service for reasons the import paperwork describes as \"performance variation,\" which is a phrase that has been interpreted many different ways and all of them are correct. The trigger mechanism is stiff in cold weather and unpredictable in warm weather, the bolt retention system allows lateral movement that affects accuracy in ways that are difficult to predict, and the prod is a fiberglass unit that is rated for a draw weight higher than the trigger mechanism was designed to manage. It shoots bolts forward, most of the time.",
    "Street Fab \"Splinter\"": "The Splinter was named either for what it does to bolts on a bad day or what it will eventually do to itself, and either interpretation applies. It is a Night City-built crossbow with a wooden stock that was not kiln-dried before use, a prod of laminated wood strips that has a slight warp that deflects shots left, and a trigger mechanism that releases at an inconsistent pull weight because the sear engagement has been set by eye rather than measurement. At close range it kills things, which is the only metric being applied.",
    "Budget Arms \"Snap\"": "Budget Arms produced the Snap as their crossbow market entry and spent approximately the right amount of effort on it for the price. The prod is a flat spring steel unit that is functional but produces considerable felt vibration on release, the stock is a polymer that creaks under load in a way that experienced crossbow shooters find deeply uncomfortable, and the string that ships with the weapon is a twisted nylon cord that Budget Arms recommends replacing before first use, which raises questions about why it was included. The string replacement Budget Arms recommends is not included.",
    "Discount Fletch \"One-Shot\"": "The One-Shot is named for its reliability profile rather than any design feature. It is Discount Fletch's budget pistol crossbow, a compact frame crossbow in a plastic that appears to have been specified by price per kilogram rather than by any mechanical property, with a trigger mechanism whose engagement can be described charitably as \"consistent with the price point.\" It fires one bolt per interaction, which is what a crossbow is supposed to do, and then requires adjustment, inspection, or occasionally replacement, which is what \"One-Shot\" means in practice.",
    "Rostovic \"Strela\"": "Strela means arrow, and Rostovic's crossbow is named with characteristic literalness for what it fires rather than any aspiration about what it is. The Strela is a compact repeating crossbow with a polymer stock, a machined aluminum rail, and a cocking mechanism that requires no external tool -- the foregrip serves as the cocking lever through a ratchet arrangement that is cleverer than the price suggests. It runs on no batteries, requires no electronic infrastructure, and functions in conditions where powered weapons become unreliable, which is why it appears in Badlands and fringe territory loadouts more often than the city market numbers would predict.",
    "Sternmeyer CB-12": "The CB-12 is Sternmeyer's crossbow, a full-size repeating unit built to the same tolerances as their firearms on the theory that a precision instrument does not become less of one because it fires bolts rather than bullets. The rail is machined steel, the trigger mechanism is a two-stage unit with an adjustable second stage, and the stock is an adjustable folding configuration in a polymer that feels more substantial than it appears. It is heavier than competitors and shoots better than the weight penalty requires, which is the Sternmeyer way.",
    "Cascade Arms \"Falcon\"": "The Falcon is a compact pistol crossbow designed for concealment, built on a reinforced polymer frame small enough to carry under a long coat. The draw weight is limited by the size of the limbs, making it less powerful than full-size crossbows, but at close range this is an acceptable tradeoff for a weapon that doesn't exist until it's needed. The trigger mechanism is single-stage with no safety -- Cascade Arms' position being that a concealment weapon whose safety requires a conscious operation is a concealment weapon that may arrive one step late.",
    "Darra Polytechnic \"Spine\"": "The Spine is Darra Polytechnic's tactical crossbow, a mid-size repeating unit designed to be legal in jurisdictions where firearms are regulated but archery equipment is not -- a gap in the law that Darra Polytechnic identified and built a product line around. The frame is a lightweight composite, the magazine holds five bolts in a side-fed configuration, and the integrated scope mount accepts any standard rail optic. It is technically a sporting implement in fourteen administrative regions of Night City, which is useful information.",
    "Nomad Rail Mk.II": "The Nomad Rail is a crossbow that appears improvised and functions precisely, a combination that is characteristic of the best Badlands engineering. The stock is a section of vehicle strut channel, the prod is fiberglass strip salvaged from wind turbine materials, and the trigger mechanism is machined from a vehicle door latch component by someone who understood what they needed it to do. The Mk.II designation distinguishes it from the original Nomad Rail, which had a trigger problem that the Mk.II has resolved. The resolution is elegant.",
    "Sternmeyer CB-12 \"Praezision\"": "The Praezision is the CB-12 rebuilt as a precision instrument, Sternmeyer's custom shop answer to the question of what a crossbow can achieve if cost is treated as a secondary concern. The trigger mechanism is a hand-fitted two-stage unit with a break weight that Sternmeyer specifies to the gram and guarantees in writing, the rail is surface-ground to a flatness specification borrowed from their rifle receiver production, and the prod is a carbon-fiber composite unit tested to a draw weight higher than the specification to verify the safety margin. The glass-smooth trigger pull is the feature that owners mention first, every time.",
    "Cascade Arms \"Falcon-X\"": "The Falcon-X is the premium Falcon platform, the concealment crossbow built for clients who need that capability and will not accept a compromise on performance to achieve it. The frame is a carbon-fiber reinforced polymer, the prod is a short-draw carbon composite unit that achieves full-size crossbow power within a pistol crossbow footprint through a cam system borrowed from Cascade's compound bow line, and the integrated optics mount accepts a micro red dot that co-witnesses with the fixed irons. It is the smallest serious crossbow available from any manufacturer.",
    "Darra Polytechnic \"Spine Elite\"": "The Spine Elite is the top of Darra Polytechnic's crossbow line, built when a client's requirements exceed what the standard Spine can deliver. The prod is a hand-selected carbon composite unit individually tested and certified for draw weight consistency, the trigger mechanism has been replaced with a match unit that Darra had built to specification by a trigger manufacturer who normally works on target rifles, and the stock is an adjustable chassis type in aluminum with a cheekpiece that positions the shooter's eye consistently with the sight plane. It is, by a significant margin, the most accurate crossbow available for purchase.",
    "Custom Works \"Quarrel\"": "The Quarrel is built by a Night City craftsperson who makes approximately four crossbows per year and names all of them the same thing, which they describe as a branding decision and which everyone else suspects is a pun they are not yet ready to retire. Each Quarrel is different in construction but identical in philosophy: the trigger is the best trigger mechanism the builder can make, the prod is the best prod available for the draw weight specified by the client, and the stock is fitted to the shooter's dimensions with a precision that the builder achieves through a process involving chalk, a shoulder imprint, and more measuring tools than the client expected. The name is a pun. The weapon is not.",
    "Thornwood \"Ironwood Series\"": "The Ironwood is built by the same hands that make the Thornwood bows, applying the same philosophy to a crossbow platform with results that the local archery community discusses in tones usually reserved for significant events. The stock is a laminated hardwood and carbon-fiber composite, the prod is a custom-built recurve unit in carbon fiber, and the trigger mechanism is a design the builder developed independently over several years of unsatisfied searching for an existing option that met their standard. It is heavier than most crossbows and more accurate than any of them.",
    # --- Assault Rifle ---
    "Budget Arms \"Warzone\"": "The Warzone is Budget Arms' assault rifle, a name that dramatically oversells a weapon built to a price point that required eliminating several components that military specifications consider load-bearing. The gas system is tuned loose to accept a wide range of ammunition, which is diplomatic language for \"we couldn't afford to be precise,\" and the barrel is a chrome-lined unit that is chrome-lined because the underlying steel required it. It cycles, it fires, and at close range it is a fully functional automatic weapon, which is the floor for what it needs to be.",
    "Dai Lung AR-3 \"Copycat\"": "The Copycat is a reproduction of the Rostovic AK-77 that was itself derived from a design that has been reproduced so many times the lineage is a philosophical question. The receiver is a pressed steel unit with visible seam lines, the furniture is a plastic that flexes under the shooting hand grip, and the bore dimensions are correct within tolerances that are wider than the original specification. At close range this does not matter. At the ranges where it would matter, nobody is using a Copycat.",
    "CCMMC Type-7 \"Surplus\"": "The Type-7 is military surplus stock sold by the crate through import channels that the procurement documentation describes as \"direct factory clearance.\" The practical meaning of this is that each crate contains rifles of varying quality, wear, and history, all of which arrive with a thin layer of cosmoline and zero accompanying documentation. Some are functional with cleaning and inspection. Some require parts. A few require the kind of optimism that parts cannot supply. Buyers sort through the crate and work with what they find.",
    "Street Forge \"Rattler\"": "The Rattler is a Night City-fabricated assault rifle assembled from a combination of purpose-built components and repurposed materials, united by the ambition of the builder and the available tooling. The receiver is a flat-fold steel construction that can be assembled and disassembled with a flat-blade screwdriver, the barrel is a replacement unit from a different caliber that has been rechambered, and the gas system is a direct-impingement arrangement that requires cleaning after every hundred rounds or it begins to Rattle in earnest. The name is onomatopoeic and was applied by the builder without apparent irony.",
    "Gutter Works \"Trench Broom\"": "The Trench Broom is named for an obsolete concept -- the close-quarters automatic weapon used to clear enclosed spaces -- and it is named correctly, because beyond close quarters its accuracy and reliability become aspirational rather than guaranteed. It is a Night City shadow-economy rifle built on a form factor that prioritizes compact size over everything else, with a barrel of legally ambiguous length, a skeleton stock of bent steel rod, and a fire control group sourced from parts that were not designed to work together but have been made to work together through a process the builder describes as \"adjustment.\" At room distance, it is effective.",
    "Militech M-76 \"Sentinel\"": "The Sentinel is the assault rifle that Militech distributes to corporate infantry, PMC grunts, and anyone else who signs a security contract above a certain tier. It is a gas-operated semi/full automatic in a flat dark earth polymer with a sixteen-inch barrel, a four-position stock, and a quad-rail handguard that ships without accessories because Militech assumes the buyer knows what they want on it. Hundreds of thousands exist across Night City and the surrounding region, making parts, magazines, and competent gunsmiths universally available, which is the most practical form of reliability.",
    "Arasaka HJSH-22 \"Nobunaga\"": "The Nobunaga is named for a warlord, and the product team at Arasaka did not choose the name casually: Nobunaga was known for combining technological advantage with overwhelming force, which is the thesis statement of this rifle. It is a bullpup configuration in Arasaka's matte anthracite, with a sixteen-inch barrel in a twenty-inch profile, ambidextrous controls, and a fire selector with a three-round burst mode that Arasaka's documentation describes as \"command decision support.\" It is expensive for a standard-quality rifle, and it earns the price.",
    "Constitutional Arms \"Minuteman\"": "The Minuteman is Constitutional Arms' assault rifle, built on an American direct-impingement platform and marketed with the kind of copy that quotes historical documents in the first paragraph. It functions reliably, accepts standard magazines and accessories, and is built to a specification that Constitutional Arms describes as \"field-ready\" and that competent gunsmiths describe as \"adequate.\" It is priced for the mid-tier market and performs at the mid-tier standard, which means it works correctly under most conditions and requires maintenance under the rest.",
    "Rostovic AK-77 \"Udar\"": "The Udar is Rostovic's assault rifle, a gas-operated rotating-bolt design in the Eastern European tradition -- simple, robust, and indifferent to conditions that would give other rifles pause. The receiver is stamped steel, the furniture is a dense black polymer, the fire selector is the original long-throw design that requires full hand repositioning to operate, and none of this is accidental: the Udar is built to be used by people who may not have had ideal training, in conditions that are not ideal, and to continue working anyway. It does.",
    "Federated Arms Tech-Assault VII": "The Tech-Assault VII is the seventh iteration of Federated Arms' budget assault rifle line, which has been continuously revised since the first Tech-Assault because each version found new ways to be approximately adequate. The VII is the best of the series: a polymer-receiver gas-operated semi/full-auto that accepts most standard accessories and fails infrequently enough that experienced users have developed a comfortable relationship with its particular quirks. It is marketed to security contractors who have a rifle budget and a slightly larger concern budget, and it meets both adequately.",
    "Arasaka HJSH-22 \"Nobunaga Custom\"": "The Nobunaga Custom is what the standard Nobunaga becomes when Arasaka's internal armory builds it for a field executive who will personally carry it. The barrel is a match-grade unit hand-lapped to the bolt face, the trigger mechanism has been replaced with a match unit from Arasaka's precision rifle division, and the gas system has been tuned for the specific ammunition the user specifies. Each Custom is accompanied by a certificate of armory inspection signed by the armorer who built it, and by a test target showing the five-round group that Arasaka considers the minimum acceptable standard for release -- a standard higher than most manufacturers' advertised specifications.",
    "Militech M-76 \"Sentinel-S\"": "The Sentinel-S is the special operations variant of the standard Sentinel, produced in limited numbers for Militech's Tier-1 contractor support program. The barrel is a match-grade cold-hammer-forged unit with improved rifling geometry, the trigger has been replaced with a Militech two-stage match unit, and the gas system has been redesigned for suppressor compatibility. The furniture has been replaced with an adjustable stock and an M-LOK handguard in a flat black Cerakote, and the whole package arrives in a Pelican case with suppressor, five magazines, and a test target. It is a serious weapon built for serious applications.",
    "Constitutional Arms \"Minuteman Elite\"": "The Minuteman Elite is Constitutional Arms' custom shop flagship rifle, built when a client specifically requests the best version of the Minuteman platform that can be made. The receiver is matched and fitted, the barrel is a match-grade stainless unit with a competition crown, and the trigger is a two-stage match assembly that Constitutional Arms' custom shop builds in-house to a specification they will quote in writing. The furniture is premium: adjustable stock, free-float handguard, and a muzzle brake that Constitutional Arms designs specifically for this configuration. The brochure still quotes historical documents. The rifle has transcended the brochure.",
    "Tsunami Arms Type-12 \"Kamikaze\"": "The Kamikaze is Tsunami's premium assault rifle, named for a word that has accumulated considerable weight over the centuries and that Tsunami's product team applied with the understanding that it will either intimidate or honor the weapon, depending on the buyer's interpretation. The frame is Tsunami's carbon-fiber polymer, the barrel is a match unit with their proprietary fluting, and the fire control group is borrowed from their competition rifle line -- a single-stage trigger that breaks with a precision that Tsunami publishes and that users consistently describe as the best trigger on any rifle they have handled. The name is either ironic or a promise. The weapon has no opinion.",
    "Custom Works \"Ironclad\"": "The Ironclad is a Night City bespoke commission rifle built by a craftsperson who came to custom rifle work from competitive shooting and has never lost the competitive shooter's intolerance for imprecision. The receiver is hand-fitted to the barrel extension, the bolt is trued, the chamber is cut by the builder to a specification they developed themselves over years of testing, and the trigger is a custom design with a pull weight that is specified by the client and delivered within five percent. The name comes from the builder's guarantee: if it does not shoot to specification, they fix it. There is no timeline on this guarantee.",
    # --- Sniper Rifle ---
    "Budget Arms \"Long Shot\"": "The Long Shot is Budget Arms' sniper rifle, a product category they entered because someone in their product development department apparently had not encountered the concept of staying in one's lane. The barrel is a heavy-profile unit of ambiguous steel specification, the action is a bolt-type design with more play than is ideal for precision work, and the included scope is a 4x fixed-power unit whose adjustments are not repeatable in the direction that matters for long-range shooting. The name acknowledges the accuracy expectations while technically being a product description.",
    "Street Forge \"Pipe Dream\"": "The Pipe Dream is a single-shot bolt-action rifle built in Night City's shadow economy from components that the builder gathered with ambition and assembled with optimism. The receiver is a machined steel cylinder, the barrel is a length of heavy-wall steel tube that has been drilled to caliber and lapped to approximate rifling, and the stock is a wooden affair built from reclaimed lumber that has been shaped with hand tools. It achieves accuracy within the limitations of its construction, which are significant but not, at moderate ranges, disqualifying.",
    "Dai Lung SR-2 \"Farshot\"": "The Farshot is Dai Lung's sniper rifle, a weapon they apparently built by taking their standard bolt-action hunting rifle and fitting it with a longer barrel and a lower-cost scope. The barrel is longer than the standard model and produces improved velocity, but the crown has not been re-cut and the rifling at the muzzle end has been disturbed by the extension process in a way that affects consistency. The included scope is a variable-power unit whose optics are visually acceptable and mechanically imprecise. At long ranges, the combination produces groups that a precise shooter finds frustrating and an imprecise shooter finds consistent with their expectations.",
    "CCMMC \"Surplus Precision\"": "The documentation that arrived with this shipment of rifles uses the word \"precision\" three times in the first paragraph, which experienced buyers have learned to interpret as a warning rather than a description. The CCMMC surplus precision rifle is a bolt-action design built to a military specification that the production run appears to have treated as a suggestion, with receivers that vary in their headspace, triggers that vary in their pull weight, and barrels that vary in ways that the importer's testing could not fully characterize within a reasonable time investment. Some of them are actually quite good. Most are consistent.",
    "Gutter Works \"Reach\"": "The Reach is what Night City's shadow economy produces when someone needs a long-range weapon option and cannot access the normal market. It is a single-shot bolt-action built on a receiver machined in a small shop from a steel specification chosen by availability, with a barrel sourced from a supplier who provides components to several of Night City's less official manufacturing operations, fitted to the receiver with a threading operation that was performed correctly. At the ranges where precision matters, the Reach asks more of its shooter than the shooter should have to give a weapon. At moderate ranges it is honest about what it can achieve.",
    "Tsunami Arms \"Karasu\"": "The Karasu -- crow -- is Tsunami's mid-tier sniper rifle, a semi-automatic precision platform in their standard dark grey with a twenty-four-inch fluted barrel, an adjustable stock, and a chassis that accepts any standard rail optic without adapter. The crow is a patient, perceptive animal that strikes without announcing itself, and Tsunami's product team applied the name correctly: the Karasu is not the most powerful rifle in its class, but it is consistent, accurate, and does not require the shooter to compensate for the weapon. It is the choice of working professionals with a realistic budget.",
    "Militech M-210 \"Longbow\"": "The Longbow is Militech's standard precision rifle, a semi-automatic platform built for corporate overwatch roles and long-range security applications. It is a large, purposeful weapon -- twenty-six inches of barrel in a chassis that adjusts for length of pull and cheek height -- with a Militech-standard rail that accepts their optics package without additional mounting hardware. It is not the most accurate semi-automatic precision rifle on the market, and it is not priced like it is. It is accurate enough to do the job it was designed for, and Militech built enough of them that doing the job is always possible.",
    "Sternmeyer SR-55": "The SR-55 is Sternmeyer's bolt-action sniper rifle, a weapon that the company approaches with the same lack of dramatic flair they apply to all their products and that achieves, through the same mechanism, a result that speaks for itself. The receiver is a cylindrical action machined from tool steel, the barrel is a match-grade medium-heavy unit in a fluted configuration, and the trigger is a two-stage adjustable unit built to the same standard as their target rifle components. It is a rifle for people who value the shot over the weapon, which is the correct hierarchy.",
    "Arasaka \"Izanagi\"": "Izanagi is a creator deity in Japanese mythology, and Arasaka named this rifle for the creator because it is a weapon for people who make decisions about who continues to exist. It is a semi-automatic precision rifle in Arasaka's matte anthracite, with a twenty-four-inch fluted barrel, an adjustable folding stock, and a fire control group that Arasaka describes as \"precision-grade\" and that operators describe as the best semi-automatic trigger available at this price point. It is a serious rifle in a serious package and it earns both.",
    "Rostovic DM-12 \"Lovac\"": "Lovac means hunter, which is the simplest possible name for a single-shot precision rifle and therefore the most Rostovic name possible. The DM-12 is a bolt-action design in a heavy bull barrel configuration, chambered in a cartridge that Rostovic selected for terminal performance over precision characteristics, because the Rostovic position on sniper rifles is that hitting the target and stopping the target should both be resolved in one round. The stock is a dense black polymer, the trigger is a two-stage unit that works correctly, and the whole rifle is unlovely in a way that does not diminish its function.",
    "Tsunami Arms \"Karasu Custom\"": "The Karasu Custom is the production Karasu rebuilt as a precision instrument by Tsunami's internal armory, the version that their field operations personnel carry when accuracy is not negotiable. The barrel is individually tested and certified for harmonic characteristics before installation, the chassis has been rebuilt with a bedding compound that eliminates the small tolerances in the production fit, and the trigger mechanism has been replaced with a unit from Tsunami's competition division. Each Custom ships with a test target showing the group Tsunami used to certify it for release, and the groups are worth examining.",
    "Militech M-210 \"Longbow-S\"": "The Longbow-S is the special operations variant of the standard Longbow, produced in limited numbers for Militech's precision overwatch program. The barrel is a match-grade heavy unit with a suppressor adapter machined into the muzzle, the action has been trued by Militech's armory, and the trigger mechanism is a two-stage match unit with a first stage adjusted to a weight that the armorer notes on the specification card in the case. It ships with a Militech suppressor, four magazines, and a test target certified to a standard that Militech's regular production cannot meet.",
    "Sternmeyer SR-55 \"Meisterwerk\"": "A Meisterwerk is a masterpiece, and Sternmeyer reserved this designation for a single rifle per year, built by their senior rifle armorer at the end of the production calendar from the best components available in their inventory. The action is trued by hand, the barrel is selected from the top of the lot testing results and hand-lapped to the bolt face, and the trigger is adjusted to a specification the armorer determines based on their judgment rather than a published number. It ships with a target, a certificate, and a letter from the armorer. The waiting list is not publicized. If you are eligible to be on it, you already know how to find out.",
    "Arasaka \"Izanagi-X\"": "The Izanagi-X is the version of the Izanagi that Arasaka's internal long-range elimination teams carry, built by the armory to individual operator specifications and never available through any external channel. The action is hand-trued to the barrel, the barrel is a match unit selected for the operator's preferred cartridge and hand-lapped at the armory, and the chassis is adjusted for the operator's dimensions in a session that Arasaka's armory documentation describes as a \"fitting.\" Each Izanagi-X is registered to the operator who carries it and is recalled and rebuilt when that operator leaves service. The armorer who builds it signs the test target.",
    "Custom Works \"Patience\"": "The waiting list for a Patience is real and runs to eighteen months, which the builder considers appropriate because the name applies to the rifle's construction as much as to its use. The action is trued from a billet receiver, the barrel is ordered to the builder's specification from a manufacturer who makes barrels for national-level competition, and the trigger is a custom design that the builder has been refining for fifteen years and considers not yet final. Each Patience is different because each client is different, and the builder's first question in every commission process is not about caliber or barrel length but about what the shooter is trying to do and why they think a rifle is the answer. The rifle that results from that conversation is always correct.",
    # --- Shotgun ---
    "Budget Arms \"Doorstop\"": "The Doorstop is Budget Arms' budget shotgun, which required them to take their already minimal Splatter platform and find areas where further cost reduction was possible. The extractor is unreliable enough that empties sometimes require manual removal, the choke is fixed at a setting that produces a pattern optimized for no particular range, and the frame has a slight bow in it that becomes relevant if you attempt to mount any accessory. It does, as Budget Arms will tell you, only need to fire once or twice, and the loud mechanical action of racking a shell into the chamber functions adequately as a deterrent regardless of what happens afterward.",
    "Street Forge \"Blunderbuss\"": "The Night City resale market named this weapon for an antique, and the comparison is more accurate than flattering. It is a single-shot percussion-cap-style shotgun built from a steel tube, a percussion mechanism, and a stock of reclaimed wood that has been shaped to shoulder without being shaped to comfort. The choke is none -- the bore terminates in a bell-mouthed flare that spreads the shot pattern immediately and dramatically. At close range this is sufficient. At any other range it is an argument for having a second weapon.",
    "CCMMC SG-1 \"Scatter\"": "The SG-1 is a Chinese military surplus pump-action shotgun that arrived in the civilian market after failing acceptance testing, which for a shotgun requires a specific kind of failure because the accuracy bar is lower than for most firearms. The pump action has a binding tendency under rapid operation, the trigger requires a reset pull that is longer than the action length suggests it should be, and the pattern produced by the fixed choke is wider than the specification calls for. None of this is disqualifying at the ranges where shotguns are used, which is presumably why it was sold rather than destroyed.",
    "Dai Lung SG-3 \"Buckshot Special\"": "The Buckshot Special is the only shotgun in Dai Lung's catalog, a product they entered because the shotgun market requires less precision than the pistol market and Dai Lung's manufacturing capabilities are better suited to less precision. The receiver is a polymer unit reinforced with steel inserts at the stress points, the barrel is a fixed-choke design in a length that passes legal muster in the markets where Dai Lung sells it, and the pump action cycles correctly when the operator applies consistent force and incorrect when they don't. The \"Special\" in the name is unexplained by any documentation Dai Lung has published.",
    "Gutter Works \"Noisemaker\"": "The Noisemaker is a Night City-fabricated single-shot shotgun whose primary tactical value, as its builder apparently understood when naming it, is acoustic rather than ballistic. The muzzle report at close range is significant enough to be disorienting in enclosed spaces regardless of whether the round impacts usefully, and the muzzle flash is substantial. The round does impact usefully at very close range. At any distance requiring aim, the Noisemaker asks for a level of precision from a weapon that was not built to provide it.",
    "Militech M-60 \"Bulldozer\"": "The Bulldozer is Militech's combat shotgun, a pump-action design built for breaching, close-quarters attrition, and situations where the intent is to be definitive rather than precise. It has a pistol grip, a folding stock, and a seven-round tube magazine, and it is heavy enough in the hand to remind the user of what they're holding before every shot. Militech sells it to site security contracts and anyone else who signs the right paperwork, and it appears in corporate corridors and Badlands checkpoints with approximately equal frequency.",
    "Rostovic DB-4 \"Ulaz\"": "Ulaz means entrance or breach, and the name describes the primary application of a double-barrel shotgun in Night City more accurately than any technical specification could. The DB-4 is a break-action over-under in a matte black, with external hammers, a robust extractor, and a build quality that Rostovic describes as \"service grade\" and that means it will function correctly through conditions that would compromise more refined designs. It is not elegant and it does not need to be.",
    "Constitutional Arms \"Gatekeeper\"": "Constitutional Arms marketed the Gatekeeper to the private property protection demographic with copy that strongly implies the weapon's primary purpose is defending a home from intruders, which is technically accurate and technically the least interesting application anyone has found for it. It is a semi-automatic shotgun with a five-round detachable magazine, a pistol grip, and a muzzle brake that reduces felt recoil to a level that Constitutional Arms' brochure calls \"manageable\" and shooters call \"honest.\" It is sold legally in most jurisdictions, which those jurisdictions have considered and decided is their position.",
    "Budget Arms \"Splatter\"": "The Splatter is named with the directness that Budget Arms applies to their entire product line: the name is the performance description, the performance description is accurate, and no further documentation is required. It is a single-shot break-action in a matte black polymer, with a fixed choke, an exposed hammer, and a barrel length that is technically legal in the jurisdictions where Budget Arms sells it, most of the time. One shot, significant effect, reload required. Budget Arms considers this a feature.",
    "Sternmeyer SG-20": "The SG-20 is Sternmeyer's semi-automatic combat shotgun, a gas-operated design with a six-round detachable box magazine and an adjustable choke system that the documentation suggests using and that most owners ignore in favor of the fixed setting. The receiver is an aluminum alloy, the barrel is a chrome-lined unit, and the whole assembly has the dense, purposeful quality that Sternmeyer achieves by not including anything ornamental. It is a reliable combat shotgun without any characteristic that makes it memorable, which is exactly what Sternmeyer intends.",
    "Militech M-60 \"Bulldozer-S\"": "The Bulldozer-S is what Militech's armory produces when a special operations contract specifies a shotgun capable of meeting a standard that the production Bulldozer doesn't hit. The barrel is a match-grade unit with an extended forcing cone that improves pattern consistency, the magazine tube has been extended, and the action has been slicked by Militech's armorers to a cycling speed that the standard model cannot achieve. The stock is a Magpul adjustable unit, the rail is full-length, and the whole package arrives with three choke tubes -- cylinder, improved cylinder, and modified -- and the tool to swap them. It is the shotgun built for people to whom a shotgun is a primary weapon rather than a backup plan.",
    "Sternmeyer SG-20 \"Rammstein\"": "The Rammstein is the premium SG-20, Sternmeyer's competition and precision combat variant, and it is one of the few Sternmeyer products with a name that carries any drama. The gas system has been redesigned by Sternmeyer's engineers for improved cycling with a wider range of ammunition, including lower-pressure loads that the standard SG-20 does not feed reliably, the barrel is a match unit with a porting pattern that Sternmeyer designed and tested over two years, and the trigger has been rebuilt with a shorter reset. It is the best-shooting semi-automatic combat shotgun available from a production manufacturer.",
    "Constitutional Arms \"Gatekeeper Elite\"": "The Gatekeeper Elite is Constitutional Arms' custom shop combat shotgun, built for clients who specified the Gatekeeper and then asked what more money would do. The receiver is hand-fitted, the barrel is a match-grade unit with a lengthened forcing cone and a competition choke system, and the trigger mechanism has been replaced with a match unit that Constitutional Arms' custom shop builds exclusively for this configuration. The walnut stock is optional and most clients choose it, because the Gatekeeper Elite is the kind of weapon people carry at a level where aesthetics are not considered frivolous.",
    "Rostovic DB-6 \"Grmljavina\"": "Grmljavina means thunderstorm, and Rostovic's premium shotgun earns the name in a way their budget products approach only aspirationally. The DB-6 is a side-by-side double-barrel built on a receiver machined rather than cast, with external hammers of a quality and fit that Rostovic's standard production cannot replicate, and barrels that are regulated at the factory -- meaning that the point of impact from each barrel is tested to confirm they shoot to the same point at a specified distance, a process that most double-barrel manufacturers skip. The finish is Rostovic's best, which is to say it is blued steel over fitted wood, and it is genuinely beautiful.",
    "Custom Works \"Last Argument\"": "The Last Argument is a Night City bespoke commission shotgun built for a client who had very specific requirements and was willing to wait and pay for them to be met. The receiver is machined from an aircraft aluminum billet, the barrel is a custom semi-automatic gas-operated unit built by a fabricator who normally makes components for competition clay shooters, and the trigger mechanism is a custom design with a pull that the builder specifies after a conversation with the client rather than before. The name was the client's contribution to the project. The builder's contribution was a weapon that fully justifies it.",
    # --- Grenade Launcher ---
    "Street Forge \"Thumper\"": "The Thumper is a Night City-fabricated single-shot grenade launcher built by someone who understood that the primary engineering challenge is containing the propellant gases long enough for the round to leave the barrel, and who resolved this challenge with a steel tube, a reinforced breech, and optimism. The trigger mechanism is a repurposed firearm component fitted to the launcher by an adapter that is either clever or reckless depending on the round count. It fires standard grenades forward with sufficient velocity to arm the fuse. The name comes from the sound it makes, which is notably different from what a properly manufactured launcher sounds like.",
    "Budget Arms \"Boom Box\"": "The Boom Box is Budget Arms' grenade launcher, a product that exists because someone in their product development department asked whether any of their existing manufacturing processes could be adapted to produce one and the answer was \"probably.\" The result is a single-shot break-action in polymer and stamped steel with a barrel that is technically the correct caliber and a trigger spring that is frequently described by users as \"optimistic\" because it requires more travel than the short mechanism should allow. It fires the grenade. The grenade does what grenades do. Budget Arms considers the product a success.",
    "CCMMC GL-1 \"Surplus Fire\"": "The GL-1 is a Chinese military surplus grenade launcher that the procurement board declined for service use after a testing period whose results Towa Manufacturing describes in the Night Market Index as \"noted.\" The safety is a rotating collar that can be dislodged by impact, a characteristic that the importer's documentation addresses with a recommendation to carry the launcher unloaded, which reduces its utility in the applications where a grenade launcher is needed. The launcher functions correctly when loaded and deliberately fired. The emphasis on deliberately is not ornamental.",
    "Gutter Works \"Lobber\"": "The Lobber is a Night City shadow-economy grenade launcher built on the principle that a tube of the correct diameter and sufficient structural integrity is most of what a grenade launcher needs to be. The barrel is a heavy-wall steel tube cut to the correct length and threaded at the breech for a trigger unit that was sourced from a different weapon category and adapted. The arc of the projectile is, as the name acknowledges, determined by physics rather than by the shooter's intention, and experienced operators develop a relationship with this specific weapon's specific ballistic behavior through a process they describe as \"experimentation\" and their fixers describe as \"incident reports.\"",
    "No-Name \"Punt Gun\"": "The name was applied by Night City's resale market to describe any grenade launcher of such poor quality that comparing it to an antique waterfowl cannon is considered accurate rather than harsh. The specific specimen most commonly seen is a single-shot break-action built from components that appear to have been designed for different applications, assembled with fasteners that are visible on the outside of the receiver and that should not be. It fires standard grenades forward. That this is the complete list of confirmed capabilities is the most honest thing that can be said about the category.",
    "Towa Manufacturing Type-K": "The Type-K is a compact single-shot break-action grenade launcher, the budget end of an already limited market. It is a straightforward design -- a barrel, a breech that opens for reloading, a trigger -- in a polymer frame with a folding stock that reduces the carry length to something that fits across a back. Towa Manufacturing builds it to a functional standard and sells it to anyone who can demonstrate a use case, which in Night City is a very low bar.",
    "Militech M-90 \"Hellfire\"": "The Hellfire is Militech's rotary-magazine grenade launcher, a six-shot revolver-cylinder design in a reinforced polymer frame with a folding stock and a full-length rail. It is large, heavy, and capable of delivering six grenades at a rate of fire limited only by the trigger pull, which is heavy by pistol standards and irrelevant by grenade launcher standards because the effect of six grenades delivered rapidly makes the weight of the trigger academic. Militech sells it to anyone with a heavy weapons license and a checkbook.",
    "Sternmeyer GL-7": "The GL-7 is Sternmeyer's semi-automatic grenade launcher, a gas-operated design built on the premise that indirect fire capability should be engineered with the same rigor as any other weapon system. The receiver is an aluminum chassis, the barrel is a reinforced steel unit with a Sternmeyer-standard rail, and the feed system is a detachable four-round box magazine that Sternmeyer designed to be reloaded under stress. It is a serious weapon built for people who take indirect fire seriously.",
    "Constitutional Arms \"Arbiter\"": "Constitutional Arms named their grenade launcher Arbiter because they ran out of civic language that wasn't already on a gun and apparently decided that a word meaning \"final authority\" was appropriate for a weapon that provides it. It is a single-shot pump-action launcher with a six-round tube magazine, a pistol grip, and an integrated folding stock. The pump action cycles quickly enough for a trained operator to maintain a meaningful rate of fire, and Constitutional Arms includes a rudimentary range card with the weapon, which is a thoughtful touch from a manufacturer that normally leans on patriotic imagery over practical documentation.",
    "Tsunami Arms Type-22 AGL": "The Type-22 is Tsunami's semi-automatic grenade launcher, a belted-feed design built for sustained fire support and not intended for individual carry. It is a large weapon in Tsunami's dark grey with a folding bipod and a receiver that accepts a standard tripod mount at the underside. Tsunami does not market it for personal use, which has not prevented personal use, and the Type-22's presence in Night City's black market is a source of ongoing concern to people whose job it is to be concerned about such things.",
    "Militech M-90 \"Hellfire-S\"": "The Hellfire-S is the special operations variant of the standard Hellfire, produced for Militech's precision fire support contracts and not available through standard procurement. The cylinder has been modified to accept a wider range of munition types including Militech's proprietary programmable fusing rounds, the trigger has been replaced with a match unit that reduces the pull weight to something a precision shooter can work with, and the barrel has been upgraded to a unit that accepts Militech's suppressor adapter -- a modification that does not silence a grenade launcher but reduces the signature enough to matter in certain operational contexts. It ships in a Pelican case with six loaded cylinders and a technical manual.",
    "Tsunami Arms Type-22 \"Raiden\"": "Raiden is thunder and lightning combined, and Tsunami named their premium automatic grenade launcher for the combination because the weapon produces both in sequence when operated. The Raiden is the Type-22 rebuilt by Tsunami's precision division with a redesigned feed mechanism, an improved barrel with better bore geometry, and a trigger group from Tsunami's precision semi-automatic rifle program. The improvements reduce dispersion at range by a figure Tsunami publishes in their technical catalog and that competing manufacturers have challenged without success. It is the most accurate automatic grenade launcher available from any manufacturer.",
    "Sternmeyer GL-7 \"Artillerie\"": "The Artillerie is the GL-7 rebuilt as a precision fire support instrument, Sternmeyer's answer to the question of what a grenade launcher looks like when it is taken as seriously as any other precision weapon. The barrel is a match unit built to a tighter bore specification than the standard GL-7, the feed mechanism has been redesigned for improved reliability with specialty munitions, and the trigger mechanism is a two-stage unit borrowed from Sternmeyer's precision rifle division. The elevation and windage adjustment on the integrated rail mount is calibrated to a finer increment than any competing system, and experienced indirect-fire operators describe using the Artillerie as the first time they understood what the weapon category could be.",
    "Constitutional Arms \"Arbiter Supreme\"": "The Supreme is Constitutional Arms' custom shop grenade launcher, built when a client specifies the Arbiter and asks what the full budget option looks like. The receiver is machined from a billet, the feed tube has been extended and redesigned for improved reliability, and the trigger mechanism has been replaced with a custom unit that Constitutional Arms' shop builds specifically for this configuration. The rail is full-length and precision-machined, and the elevation adjustment on the included mount is calibrated by the armorer who installs it. The name escalation from Arbiter to Arbiter Supreme is Constitutional Arms' most subtle joke, and they are not known for subtlety.",
    "Custom Works \"Final Motion\"": "The Final Motion is a Night City bespoke commission grenade launcher built for clients whose requirements for the category exceed what production manufacturers provide. The receiver is a titanium alloy chassis, the barrel is a custom unit built by a fabricator who normally produces artillery components at reduced scale, and the trigger mechanism is a custom design with an adjustable first stage and a second stage break that the builder specifies after a consultation with the client. Three have been built. The clients who own them do not discuss them, which the builder considers the appropriate response to the product.",
    # --- Rocket Launcher ---
    "Street Forge \"Tube\"": "The Tube is named with the honesty that budget weaponry occasionally achieves, because it is, in fact, a tube. It is a steel tube of the correct caliber, reinforced at the breech with additional material and fitted with a trigger mechanism sourced from a different weapon category, that will fire a standard rocket forward at sufficient velocity to arm the fuse. The sighting system is a notch filed into the muzzle end and a pin welded to the breech end. The back-blast hazard is documented only in the instruction that someone has written in marker on the tube itself: \"stand back.\" The tube does not specify how far.",
    "Budget Arms \"Firework\"": "The Firework is Budget Arms' disposable rocket launcher, a product that requires a specific kind of courage to market and a different kind to use. The tube is a polymer composite that Budget Arms sources from a supplier whose primary product is irrigation pipe, reinforced with a fiberglass winding that is thinner than the application recommends. The guidance system is the shooter's arm and the basic cross-hair sight printed on a sticker applied to the tube. It fires. The rocket goes forward. Budget Arms considers this a complete product description.",
    "Gutter Works \"One Way\"": "The One Way is a Night City-fabricated single-shot rocket launcher whose name acknowledges both the direction of the projectile and the reliability expectation for the tube after firing. It is built from heavy-wall steel pipe, fitted with a trigger mechanism of Night City shadow-economy origin, and finished with a coat of black paint that serves no protective purpose but makes the weapon look more intentional than it is. The sight is a ring-and-post arrangement welded to the tube. At the ranges where rocket launchers are relevant, the sight and the operator's judgment produce approximately equal results, and the operator's judgment is free.",
    "CCMMC RL-1 \"Surplus Boom\"": "The Surplus Boom is a Chinese military surplus rocket launcher that failed acceptance testing and was subsequently sold through channels that the importer's documentation describes as \"gray market surplus disposition.\" The seals in the rocket motor storage chamber have degraded during transit and storage in a way that the importer's inspection process did not fully characterize, and the propellant in the rockets included with the initial shipment should be treated as variable in performance. The launcher tube is structurally sound. Militech's field representatives, who have encountered the RL-1 in the field, recommend treating it as a one-shot weapon for reasons they have declined to specify in writing.",
    "No-Name \"Desperation Shot\"": "The Desperation Shot is the name applied by Night City's fixer community to any improvised rocket launcher of last-resort quality, but the most common specific example is a steel pipe fitted with a percussion primer mechanism and loaded with a rocket that has been separated from its original launcher system. The firing mechanism requires two hands to operate deliberately and one hand to operate accidentally, which is a design characteristic rather than a flaw. It fires the rocket forward with sufficient force to arm the fuse. What it does to the operator's hearing is undocumented because the operators in question are generally occupied with other concerns immediately afterward.",
    "Militech M-115 \"Javelin\"": "The Javelin is a single-shot disposable rocket launcher, a sealed polymer tube containing a rocket motor and a warhead that Militech builds, seals, and sells with the explicit expectation that it will be used once and discarded. The tube is matte black with a fold-out front grip, a flip-up rear sight, and a firing mechanism that requires two deliberate actions to prevent accidental discharge. It is accurate at the ranges it was designed for, effective against the targets it was designed against, and light enough that carrying two is not unreasonable.",
    "Militech M-120 \"Dawnbreaker\"": "The Dawnbreaker is a reloadable guided rocket launcher, a shoulder-mounted platform with a target-lock sight system that tracks vehicle-sized targets and provides launch cues when the solution is within acceptable parameters. The tube is a glass-fiber reinforced composite in a dark olive, the sighting system is a thermal-capable unit, and the rockets are Militech proprietary rounds available through their standard logistics network. It is expensive to purchase and more expensive to operate, and Militech sells it to anyone whose contract specifies anti-vehicle capability.",
    "Sternmeyer RL-20": "The RL-20 is Sternmeyer's reloadable rocket launcher, a platform built on the premise that a weapon system intended for extended operations should be capable of more than one shot before requiring replacement. The tube is a reinforced polymer, the sighting system is a simple optical unit with range compensation, and the rockets are standard 66mm rounds compatible with most European-market ammunition. Sternmeyer did not name it dramatically. Sternmeyer never names things dramatically. It works precisely as specified and that is, in Sternmeyer's view, the complete product description.",
    "KTech \"Hammerfall\"": "The Hammerfall is KTech's entry into the anti-vehicle launcher market, a product that KTech supports with aggressive pricing and a marketing campaign that leans heavily on the name and lightly on the specifications. It is a shoulder-fired rocket launcher with a basic optical sight and a guidance system that KTech describes as \"smart-seeking\" and that experienced operators describe as \"enthusiastically directional.\" At the ranges where rocket launchers are typically used, the guidance system's ambitions and its capabilities are close enough to the same thing to matter, which is KTech's position and which the product broadly supports.",
    "Constitutional Arms \"Finality\"": "Constitutional Arms named their rocket launcher Finality, which is either excellent product naming or the moment their naming team ran out of civic language and decided to be honest. It is a reloadable single-shot launcher with a break-open breech, a basic iron sight, and a construction that Constitutional Arms describes as \"durable\" and that means it will survive the handling that rocket launchers receive in field use without structural compromise. It fires standard 66mm rockets accurately at relevant ranges and does to the target what a rocket does to a target, which Constitutional Arms apparently felt required a name acknowledging the permanence of the outcome.",
    "Militech M-120 \"Dawnbreaker-S\"": "The Dawnbreaker-S is the special operations variant of the standard Dawnbreaker, produced for Militech's precision anti-armor program and not available through standard procurement channels. The guidance system has been upgraded to a multi-mode seeker head capable of laser designation, thermal imaging, and a passive RF mode that the documentation describes as useful \"in contested electronic environments,\" which is Militech's language for situations where the enemy is also using guidance jammers. The rockets are Militech proprietary programmable rounds, the tube is a lightweight carbon-fiber composite, and the whole system arrives in a case with a certification from Militech's testing range showing a hit at the maximum specified engagement range.",
    "KTech \"Hammerfall Elite\"": "The Hammerfall Elite is what KTech produces when the standard Hammerfall's guidance system is replaced with one that works correctly. The seeker head is a new-design dual-mode thermal and millimetric wave unit that KTech sourced from a contractor with genuine guidance expertise, the tube has been rebuilt in a carbon-fiber composite, and the rocket motor has been redesigned for improved range and reduced smoke signature. It retains the Hammerfall name because KTech's marketing team insisted and their engineering team was too pleased with the performance to argue. The guidance system is no longer optimistic. It is correct.",
    "Sternmeyer RL-20 \"Donnerkeil\"": "Donnerkeil means thunderbolt, and it is the only dramatic name in Sternmeyer's entire catalog, which tells you something about how seriously the RL-20's development team took this project. The tube is a carbon-fiber reinforced composite built to an aerospace standard, the sighting system is a precision optical unit with ballistic compensation for range and crosswind, and the rockets are a Sternmeyer-developed proprietary round with improved motor consistency that produces tighter hit patterns at maximum range than any competing system publishes. It is reloadable, accurate past any range at which rocket launchers are typically employed, and named for a lightning bolt, because even Sternmeyer has limits.",
    "Constitutional Arms \"Finality Plus\"": "The Finality Plus is Constitutional Arms' custom shop rocket launcher, which is a phrase that required their legal team to review before it could be included in a product name. The tube is a reinforced composite rebuilt to a higher structural standard than the production Finality, the breech mechanism has been redesigned for improved sealing and more consistent rocket positioning, and the sighting system has been replaced with a precision optical unit with adjustable elevation calibrated to Constitutional Arms' proprietary rocket specifications. The name -- Finality Plus -- is the funniest thing Constitutional Arms has ever put on a product, and they appear to be aware of this, which is new for them.",
    "Custom Works \"Extinction Event\"": "There is one Extinction Event. It was commissioned by a client whose requirements document ran to eleven pages and whose budget was not a limiting factor. The tube is a titanium alloy composite built by an aerospace fabricator, the guidance system is a custom dual-mode seeker head sourced through channels that the builder has declined to document, and the rockets are a proprietary design built to the client's specifications by a propellant contractor whose other clients are not discussed. The builder named it at the client's instruction, received the payment, and then received a second message from the client three weeks after delivery that read, in its entirety, \"correct.\" That is the complete production history of the Extinction Event.",
}
